<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chagos Archipelago</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chagos Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chagos Archipelago is a group of some sixty coral islands in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, the largest being Diego Garcia. Ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, it was detached by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1965 to form the British Indian Ocean Territory, and its inhabitants were expelled to make way for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> military base. Mauritius long disputed the separation, and in 2025 Britain agreed to transfer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> while keeping the base.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chagos-archipelago.jpg" alt="Chagos Archipelago" width="320" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohonu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chagos_map2y.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Chagos Archipelago is a group of coral atolls, reefs and submerged banks in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, at the southern end of the Chagos–Laccadive Ridge. It lies about 2,200 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> and 500 km south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>. Five atolls carry islands: Diego Garcia, Peros Banhos, the Salomon Islands, the Egmont Islands and the Great Chagos Bank, described as the world's largest atoll by area. The 55 to 59 islands are small and low, with a total land area of about 53 km2, more than half of it on Diego Garcia. The reefs are largely free of local pollution, so they serve as a scientific reference site.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Portuguese navigators probably sighted and named the islands early in the 16th century, and they appear on Portuguese charts from 1538. From the 1740s ships from Île de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> was then called, explored the islands, and concessions for coconut plantations followed, leading to permanent settlement. Accounts of the date differ: one dates it to a concession of February 1783, the territory's British administration dates the first French copra plantations to 1793. The plantations were worked by enslaved people brought mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>.</p><p>The United Kingdom administered the archipelago as a dependency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> until 1965, when it detached the islands to form the British Indian Ocean Territory. Between 1967 and 1973 the whole population, the Chagossians, was either prevented from returning or removed, most of them to Mauritius, and construction of a joint British–American military base on Diego Garcia began in March 1971. Mauritius says that the detachment was unlawful. In May 2025 the two governments signed a treaty on the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The archipelago has no permanent inhabitants. About 2,000 people, mostly military personnel and contractors, are ordinarily present on Diego Garcia. The Chagossians, also called Ilois, descend mainly from the enslaved Africans and Malagasy brought to the plantations and from labourers recruited later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>. By the end of the 19th century they had formed a distinct island society, mostly Roman Catholic. Their music and dance, Sega tambour Chagos, was inscribed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 2019 as intangible cultural heritage in need of urgent safeguarding. Their language, Chagos Kreol, is a French-lexified creole related to those of Mauritius and Seychelles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chagos Archipelago” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Chagos Archipelago</b>, detached by Britain in 1965, remained disputed.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-mauritius">British Mauritius</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1965 the United Kingdom split the <b>Chagos Archipelago</b> from Mauritius.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauritius">Mauritius</a></li></ul></section>
